<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Gamma Mixture Modeling for Cosine Similarity in Small Language Models</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Kevin Player  <a href="https://orcid.org/0009-0000-7180-7985" title="ORCID 0009-0000-7180-7985" class="ltx_ref ltx_orcid"><svg class="ltx_orcidlogo" height="1em" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 72 72" width="1em"><path style="--ltx-fill-color:#A6CE39;" d="M72,36 C72,55.884375 55.884375,72 36,72 C16.115625,72 0,55.884375 0,36 C0,16.115625 16.115625,0 36,0 C55.884375,0 72,16.115625 72,36 Z" fill="#A6CE39"></path><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" transform="translate(18.868966, 12.910345)"><polygon points="5.03734929 39.1250878 0.695429861 39.1250878 0.695429861 9.14431787 5.03734929 9.14431787 5.03734929 22.6930505 5.03734929 39.1250878"></polygon><path d="M11.409257,9.14431787 L23.1380784,9.14431787 C34.303014,9.14431787 39.2088191,17.0664074 39.2088191,24.1486995 C39.2088191,31.846843 33.1470485,39.1530811 23.1944669,39.1530811 L11.409257,39.1530811 L11.409257,9.14431787 Z M15.7511765,35.2620194 L22.6587756,35.2620194 C32.49858,35.2620194 34.7541226,27.8438084 34.7541226,24.1486995 C34.7541226,18.1301509 30.8915059,13.0353795 22.4332213,13.0353795 L15.7511765,13.0353795 L15.7511765,35.2620194 Z"></path><path d="M5.71401206,2.90182329 C5.71401206,4.441452 4.44526937,5.72914146 2.86638958,5.72914146 C1.28750978,5.72914146 0.0187670918,4.441452 0.0187670918,2.90182329 C0.0187670918,1.33420133 1.28750978,0.0745051096 2.86638958,0.0745051096 C4.44526937,0.0745051096 5.71401206,1.36219458 5.71401206,2.90182329 Z"></path></g></svg></a>
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_note"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Note: </span><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">kplayer@andrew.cmu.edu</span>
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Software Engineering Institute
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Carnegie Mellon University
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We study the cosine similarity of sentence transformer embeddings and observe that they are well modeled by gamma mixtures. From a fixed corpus, we measure similarities between all document embeddings and a reference query embedding. Empirically we find that these distributions are often well captured by a gamma distribution shifted and truncated to <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-1,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-1,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, and in many cases, by a gamma mixture. We propose a heuristic model in which a hierarchical clustering of topics naturally leads to a gamma-mixture structure in the similarity scores. Finally, we outline an expectation–maximization algorithm for fitting shifted gamma mixtures, which provides a practical tool for modeling similarity distributions.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Cosine similarity is a widely used measure in semantic search <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> and the advent of sentence transformers has driven widespread adoption of text-matching systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, where similarity scores are often used directly. In many semantic search applications, only the ranking of documents matters. In this paper, we instead focus on the statistical significance of a match. This perspective enables applications such as identifying the most surprising assignment of sentence fragments in a summary to fragments in a document, see an example in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Gamma Mixture Modeling for Cosine Similarity in Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. More generally, it can be used to compare the combined significance of multiple matches from one search result against those from another.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.05309v1/highlight_example.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:410/132;" width="410" height="132" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>The most significant matching of three sentence fragments in a summary(queries) with fragments in the document. Example from xsum dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> using pvalues modeled from <span id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_typewriter">all-MiniLM-L6-v2</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A common approach to computing a p-value is the permutation test <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, which uses an empirical distribution as the null. In the context of cosine similarity, there are many examples of this approach <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, and specifically for sentence embeddings, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>. While this technique is effective, it requires a sufficiently large dataset to accurately model the tail of the null distribution and offers limited insight into its overall shape. In this paper, we propose an alternative: an accurate modeling approach that requires far less data while providing a better representation of the tail behavior.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">There are several natural distributions to consider when modeling cosine similarities. Smith et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> study biological data with multivariate normals. The beta distribution (rescaled to <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-1,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-1,1]</annotation></semantics></math>) is another candidate, given the <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-1,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-1,1]</annotation></semantics></math> support and the algebraic form of the dot product. The von Mises–Fisher (vMF) distribution is particularly appealing, as it models a Gaussian conditioned on the unit sphere <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|x|=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|x|=1</annotation></semantics></math>, matching the normalization inherent to cosine similarity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">However, an example histogram in Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Gamma Mixture Modeling for Cosine Similarity in Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows a typical empirical distribution. It is asymmetric, with a long right tail and a nonzero mean. The normal distribution fails to capture the asymmetry, while both the beta and vMF distributions produce a heavy left tail rather than the observed right tail (see also Section <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 Von Mises-Fisher Modeling ‣ 2 Modeling Cosine Similarity ‣ Gamma Mixture Modeling for Cosine Similarity in Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>). Surprisingly, a simple gamma distribution, truncated and shifted to <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-1,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-1,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, provides an excellent fit.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.05309v1/single_gamma.png" id="S1.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:361/215;" width="361" height="215" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Example distribution <math id="S1.F2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> for the abstract ‘Using Genetic Algorithms for Texts Classification Problems’ (arXiv dataset). A shifted gamma distribution provides a good fit.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Gopal and Yang <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> introduced a hierarchical vMF mixture model for clustering. Building on this idea, we present a simplified hierarchical sampling argument that heuristically explains why cosine similarities may follow a gamma mixture distribution, see Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Hierarchical Modeling ‣ Gamma Mixture Modeling for Cosine Similarity in Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. Empirically, we find that gamma mixtures not only arise naturally from this perspective but also fit the observed data remarkably well.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Modeling Cosine Similarity ‣ Gamma Mixture Modeling for Cosine Similarity in Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we present empirical distributions and demonstrate that they are often well modeled by gamma distributions and gamma mixtures. Section <a href="#S3" title="3 (Shifted) Gamma Mixture Math ‣ Gamma Mixture Modeling for Cosine Similarity in Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> presents a formal formulation of our gamma mixture models along with the corresponding Expectation-Maximization (EM) algorithm. Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Hierarchical Modeling ‣ Gamma Mixture Modeling for Cosine Similarity in Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> presents a heuristic model based on the hierarchical clustering of topics. In Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Other Models and Data ‣ Gamma Mixture Modeling for Cosine Similarity in Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, we demonstrate that additional datasets and smaller language models are likewise well modeled by gamma distributions. Finally we finish in Section <a href="#S6" title="6 Warm Starting Speed up ‣ Gamma Mixture Modeling for Cosine Similarity in Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> with a warm start technique for our EM and some benchmarks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Modeling Cosine Similarity</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the set of all possible sentences, and let <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E:\mathcal{S}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E:\mathcal{S}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be an embedding. In this paper, we mainly use the default <span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_typewriter">BERTopic</span> sentence transformer <span id="S2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_typewriter">all-MiniLM-L6-v2</span>, <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=384" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>384</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=384</annotation></semantics></math>, based on MiniLM<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>. We focus on a <span id="S2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">topical</span> subset <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{0}\subseteq\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{0}\subseteq\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, which consists of sentences drawn from a particular domain-specific corpus, we mainly consider the arXiv abstracts dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>. Given a fixed query <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in\mathcal{S}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in\mathcal{S}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, we study the distribution <math id="S2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{q}(\mathcal{S}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{q}(\mathcal{S}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> of</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x={\bf cosine\_sim}(E(q),E(d))\text{ for }d\in\mathcal{S}_{0}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐜𝐨𝐬𝐢𝐧𝐞</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐬𝐢𝐦</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> for </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x={\bf cosine\_sim}(E(q),E(d))\text{ for }d\in\mathcal{S}_{0}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Crucially, this restriction to <math id="S2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> significantly affects the shape of <math id="S2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, the distribution tends to have a positive mean, reflecting topical coherence.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Empirically, we observe that a (<math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> - shifted) gamma distribution truncated to <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-1,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-1,1]</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(\alpha,c,\lambda)(x)=\frac{(x-c)^{\alpha-1}e^{-\lambda(x-c)}\lambda^{\alpha}}{\Gamma(\alpha)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mi>α</mi></msup></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(\alpha,c,\lambda)(x)=\frac{(x-c)^{\alpha-1}e^{-\lambda(x-c)}\lambda^{\alpha}}{\Gamma(\alpha)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">often provides a good fit to the distribution of cosine similarities (see Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Gamma Mixture Modeling for Cosine Similarity in Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>):</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S2.p3.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><math id="S2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S2.p3.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><math id="S2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S2.p3.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><math id="S2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.p3.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">13.3</td>
<td id="S2.p3.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">-0.28</td>
<td id="S2.p3.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">35.5</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The need for both a shift and truncation is surprising: cosine similarity is supported on <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-1,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-1,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, whereas the gamma distribution is supported on <math id="S2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,\infty]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,\infty]</annotation></semantics></math>. In practice this means we are modeling with the tail-truncated portion of a shifted gamma, with most of the omitted mass lying in a highly improbable region.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><img src="2510.05309v1/gamma_mixture_best.png" id="S2.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:362/229;" width="362" height="229" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><img src="2510.05309v1/gamma_mixture_summands.png" id="S2.F3.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:365/227;" width="365" height="227" alt="Refer to caption"></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>An example of a <math id="S2.F3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.F3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> in this case is the arXiv abstract for <span id="S2.F3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">‘‘Why Global Performance is a Poor Metric for Verifying Convergence of Multi-agent Learning’’</span> in the arXiv dataset. <math id="S2.F3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> is fit well by a mixture of two gamma distributions.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In cases where a single gamma distribution does not adequately capture the observed distribution, a mixture of gamma distributions often yields a better approximation. Again, the precise theoretical justification remains open, but empirical results show strong agreement with this model (see Figure <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 2 Modeling Cosine Similarity ‣ Gamma Mixture Modeling for Cosine Similarity in Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>):</p>
<table id="S2.p5.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S2.p5.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S2.p5.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S2.p5.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><math id="S2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S2.p5.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><math id="S2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S2.p5.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><math id="S2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S2.p5.2.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><math id="S2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S2.p5.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S2.p5.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">1</th>
<td id="S2.p5.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.10</td>
<td id="S2.p5.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">67.1</td>
<td id="S2.p5.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">-0.20</td>
<td id="S2.p5.2.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">109.0</td></tr>
<tr id="S2.p5.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S2.p5.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row">2</th>
<td id="S2.p5.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.90</td>
<td id="S2.p5.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">19.2</td>
<td id="S2.p5.2.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">-0.25</td>
<td id="S2.p5.2.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">45.8</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p5.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the mixture parameter over states <math id="S2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Von Mises-Fisher Modeling</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Gopal and Yang <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> model semantic similarity using von Mises-Fisher vMF distribution in <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> dimensions. The vMF distribution has density on the sphere <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|x|=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|x|=1</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)=C_{d}(\kappa)e^{\kappa\mu^{T}x}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>κ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>μ</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)=C_{d}(\kappa)e^{\kappa\mu^{T}x}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{d}(\kappa)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>κ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{d}(\kappa)</annotation></semantics></math> does not depend on <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mu|=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mu|=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Without loss of generality, we can pick <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> to be a basis vector along the first dimension, and then cosine similarity, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=\mu^{T}x\in[-1,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>μ</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=\mu^{T}x\in[-1,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, is just the first coordinate of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. We integrate along the other dimensions of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> to find a pdf for the cosine similarity <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(t)\propto(1-t^{2})^{\frac{d-3}{2}}e^{\kappa t}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(t)\propto(1-t^{2})^{\frac{d-3}{2}}e^{\kappa t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">upto a constant that depends on <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>. This distribution is centered at positive <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> but exhibits a heavy left tail, see Figure <a href="#S2.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 2.1 Von Mises-Fisher Modeling ‣ 2 Modeling Cosine Similarity ‣ Gamma Mixture Modeling for Cosine Similarity in Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, which conflicts with the right-tailed empirical behavior.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.05309v1/vmf.png" id="S2.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:286/219;" width="286" height="219" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Typical vMF distribution of cosine similarity (<math id="S2.F4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=10</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.F4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa=10</annotation></semantics></math>).</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>(Shifted) Gamma Mixture Math</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Mixture Formulation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose we are given a dataset <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, and we wish to model it as a mixture of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> shifted<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
            The model assumes that <math id="footnote1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}&gt;c_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}&gt;c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></span> gamma distributions <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\alpha_{i},c_{i},\lambda_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\alpha_{i},c_{i},\lambda_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\alpha_{i},c_{i},\lambda_{i}}(x)=\sum_{i=1}^{s}\tau_{i}G_{\alpha_{i},c_{i},\lambda_{i}}(x)=\sum_{i=1}^{s}\tau_{i}\frac{(x-c_{i})^{\alpha_{i}-1}e^{-{\lambda_{i}}(x-c_{i})}{\lambda_{i}}^{\alpha_{i}}}{\Gamma(\alpha_{i})}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>s</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>s</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mmultiscripts></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\alpha_{i},c_{i},\lambda_{i}}(x)=\sum_{i=1}^{s}\tau_{i}G_{\alpha_{i},c_{i},\lambda_{i}}(x)=\sum_{i=1}^{s}\tau_{i}\frac{(x-c_{i})^{\alpha_{i}-1}e^{-{\lambda_{i}}(x-c_{i})}{\lambda_{i}}^{\alpha_{i}}}{\Gamma(\alpha_{i})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are the mixture weights with <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum\tau_{i}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum\tau_{i}=1</annotation></semantics></math>. We can use expectation maximization (EM) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> to do this.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Expectation Maximization Setup</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It is well known how to fit Gamma mixture models using Expectation Conditional<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
            Conditional just means that we split the maximization step up into parts.</span></span></span> Maximization ECM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>. We present an version for our shifted GMMs. The expectation (E) step is to compute</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{t,i}=\texttt{Prob}\left(\begin{array}[]{c}\text{state }i\\
\text{and}\\
\text{sample }t\end{array}\right)=\tau_{i}G_{\alpha_{i},c_{i},\lambda_{i}}(x_{t})/P_{\alpha_{i},c_{i},\lambda_{i}}(x_{t})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">Prob</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mtext>state </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mtext>and</mtext></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mtext>sample </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{t,i}=\texttt{Prob}\left(\begin{array}[]{c}\text{state }i\\
\text{and}\\
\text{sample }t\end{array}\right)=\tau_{i}G_{\alpha_{i},c_{i},\lambda_{i}}(x_{t})/P_{\alpha_{i},c_{i},\lambda_{i}}(x_{t})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The maximization (M) step is more involved and will fill out the remainder of this section. We first write out the <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> weighted log likelihood function</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q(\alpha_{i},c_{i},\lambda_{i})=\log(P_{\alpha_{i},c_{i},\lambda_{i}})=\sum_{t,i}\gamma_{t,i}\left(\begin{array}[]{l}\log\tau_{i}+(\alpha_{i}-1)\log(x_{t}-c_{i})\\
-\lambda_{i}(x_{t}-c_{i})+\alpha_{i}\log\lambda_{i}-\log\Gamma(\alpha_{i})\\
\end{array}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q(\alpha_{i},c_{i},\lambda_{i})=\log(P_{\alpha_{i},c_{i},\lambda_{i}})=\sum_{t,i}\gamma_{t,i}\left(\begin{array}[]{l}\log\tau_{i}+(\alpha_{i}-1)\log(x_{t}-c_{i})\\
-\lambda_{i}(x_{t}-c_{i})+\alpha_{i}\log\lambda_{i}-\log\Gamma(\alpha_{i})\\
\end{array}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">and then take some derivatives.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Reestimating <math id="S3.SS3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compute</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial Q}{\partial\tau_{i}}=\sum_{t}\gamma_{t,i}\frac{1}{\tau_{i}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>t</mi></munder><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial Q}{\partial\tau_{i}}=\sum_{t}\gamma_{t,i}\frac{1}{\tau_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and use a Lagrange multiplier <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum\tau_{i}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum\tau_{i}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial Q}{\partial\tau_{i}}=\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial Q}{\partial\tau_{i}}=\nu</annotation></semantics></math>, to update <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\tau_{i}}=\frac{\sum_{t}\gamma_{t,i}}{\sum_{t,j}\gamma_{t,j}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\tau_{i}}=\frac{\sum_{t}\gamma_{t,i}}{\sum_{t,j}\gamma_{t,j}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Elimination of <math id="S3.SS4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To simplify the maximization of <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, we will eliminate <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Compute</p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0=\frac{\partial Q}{\partial\lambda_{i}}=\sum_{t}\gamma_{t,i}\left(-(x_{t}-c)+\frac{\alpha_{i}}{\lambda_{i}}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>t</mi></munder><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0=\frac{\partial Q}{\partial\lambda_{i}}=\sum_{t}\gamma_{t,i}\left(-(x_{t}-c)+\frac{\alpha_{i}}{\lambda_{i}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and write <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in terms of <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}=\alpha_{i}\kappa_{i}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}=\alpha_{i}\kappa_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where</p>
<table id="S3.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{i}=\frac{\sum_{t}\gamma_{t,i}}{\sum_{t}\gamma_{t,i}(x_{t}-c_{i})}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{i}=\frac{\sum_{t}\gamma_{t,i}}{\sum_{t}\gamma_{t,i}(x_{t}-c_{i})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.4" class="ltx_p">is the inverse of the <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-weighted mean<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
              
              
              
            This mirrors the structure of the rate parameter in maximum likelihood fitting of the standard gamma distribution.</span></span></span> of <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}-c_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}-c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We now plug this expression back into the Q-function to obtain a reduced form:</p>
<table id="S3.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{0}(\alpha_{i},c_{i}):=Q(\alpha_{i},c_{i},\lambda_{i}=\alpha_{i}\kappa_{i})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{0}(\alpha_{i},c_{i}):=Q(\alpha_{i},c_{i},\lambda_{i}=\alpha_{i}\kappa_{i}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.5" class="ltx_p">which we will maximize over <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in the next step.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.5 </span>Reestimating <math id="S3.SS5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="S3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We differentiate <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> to find an equation in terms of <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> having eliminated <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<table id="S3.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial Q_{0}}{\partial\alpha_{i}}=\sum_{t}\gamma_{t,i}\left(\log(x_{t}-c_{i})-\kappa_{i}(x_{t}-c_{i})+\log\alpha_{i}+1+\log\kappa_{i}-\psi(\alpha_{i})\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>t</mi></munder><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial Q_{0}}{\partial\alpha_{i}}=\sum_{t}\gamma_{t,i}\left(\log(x_{t}-c_{i})-\kappa_{i}(x_{t}-c_{i})+\log\alpha_{i}+1+\log\kappa_{i}-\psi(\alpha_{i})\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> is the digamma function. We can next compute</p>
<table id="S3.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial^{2}Q_{0}}{\partial\alpha_{i}^{2}}=\sum_{t}\gamma_{t,i}\left(\frac{1}{\alpha_{i}}-\psi^{(1)}(\alpha_{i})\right)&lt;0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>t</mi></munder><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial^{2}Q_{0}}{\partial\alpha_{i}^{2}}=\sum_{t}\gamma_{t,i}\left(\frac{1}{\alpha_{i}}-\psi^{(1)}(\alpha_{i})\right)&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where the positivity comes from a known trigamma inequality<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
              
              
              
            This is consistent with the standard gamma distribution, where the log-likelihood is also strictly concave in <math id="footnote4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> after eliminating <math id="footnote4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> for the same reason.</span></span></span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>. So equation (<a href="#S3.E14" title="In 3.5 Reestimating 𝛼_𝑖 and 𝜆_𝑖 ‣ 3 (Shifted) Gamma Mixture Math ‣ Gamma Mixture Modeling for Cosine Similarity in Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>) is monotone increasing and we can find a root <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\alpha_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\alpha_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> by bisecting it. Then we use equation (<a href="#S3.E11" title="In 3.4 Elimination of 𝜆_𝑖 ‣ 3 (Shifted) Gamma Mixture Math ‣ Gamma Mixture Modeling for Cosine Similarity in Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>) to update <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\lambda_{i}}=\widehat{\alpha_{i}}\kappa_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\lambda_{i}}=\widehat{\alpha_{i}}\kappa_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.6 </span>Reestimating <math id="S3.SS6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="S3.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we focus on <math id="S3.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<table id="S3.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial Q}{\partial c_{i}}=\sum_{t}\gamma_{t,i}\left(\frac{1-\alpha_{i}}{x_{t}-c_{i}}+\lambda_{i}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>t</mi></munder><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial Q}{\partial c_{i}}=\sum_{t}\gamma_{t,i}\left(\frac{1-\alpha_{i}}{x_{t}-c_{i}}+\lambda_{i}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS6.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and the second derivative is</p>
<table id="S3.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial^{2}Q}{\partial c_{i}^{2}}=\sum_{t}\gamma_{t,i}\frac{1-\alpha_{i}}{(x_{t}-c_{i})^{2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>t</mi></munder><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial^{2}Q}{\partial c_{i}^{2}}=\sum_{t}\gamma_{t,i}\frac{1-\alpha_{i}}{(x_{t}-c_{i})^{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS6.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S3.SS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{t,i}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{t,i}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and the denominator is always positive, the sign of the second derivative is determined entirely by <math id="S3.SS6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\alpha_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\alpha_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is fixed. Therefore, <math id="S3.SS6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> is either strictly convex, linear, or strictly concave in <math id="S3.SS6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, depending on the sign of <math id="S3.SS6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\alpha_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\alpha_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, generically<span id="footnote5" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">5</span>
              
              
              
            The linear <math id="footnote5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=1</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="footnote5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{t}\gamma_{t,i}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{t}\gamma_{t,i}=0</annotation></semantics></math> case requires no <math id="footnote5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> update.</span></span></span>, the equation (<a href="#S3.E16" title="In 3.6 Reestimating 𝑐_𝑖 ‣ 3 (Shifted) Gamma Mixture Math ‣ Gamma Mixture Modeling for Cosine Similarity in Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>) has at most one solution, and we can find the root <math id="S3.SS6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{c_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{c_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> efficiently using a bisection method.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.7 </span>Non-convexity of <math id="S3.SS7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{0}</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="S3.SS7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Although it might be desirable to jointly reestimate <math id="S3.SS7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{0}(\alpha_{i},c_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{0}(\alpha_{i},c_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, the Hessian for the <math id="S3.SS7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-th summand</p>
<table id="S3.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{array}[]{ll}H&amp;=\begin{bmatrix}\frac{\partial^{2}Q}{\partial\alpha_{i}^{2}}\frac{\partial^{2}Q}{\partial\alpha_{i}c_{i}}\\
\frac{\partial^{2}Q}{\partial\alpha_{i}c_{i}}\frac{\partial^{2}Q}{\partial c_{i}^{2}}\\
\end{bmatrix}\\
&amp;=\begin{bmatrix}\frac{1}{\alpha_{i}}-\psi^{(1)}(\alpha_{i})&amp;-\frac{1}{x_{t}-c_{i}}+\kappa_{i}\\
-\frac{1}{x_{t}-c_{i}}+\kappa_{i}&amp;\frac{1-\alpha_{i}}{(x_{t}-c_{i})^{2}}\\
\end{bmatrix}\end{array}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mi>H</mi></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{array}[]{ll}H&amp;=\begin{bmatrix}\frac{\partial^{2}Q}{\partial\alpha_{i}^{2}}\frac{\partial^{2}Q}{\partial\alpha_{i}c_{i}}\\
\frac{\partial^{2}Q}{\partial\alpha_{i}c_{i}}\frac{\partial^{2}Q}{\partial c_{i}^{2}}\\
\end{bmatrix}\\
&amp;=\begin{bmatrix}\frac{1}{\alpha_{i}}-\psi^{(1)}(\alpha_{i})&amp;-\frac{1}{x_{t}-c_{i}}+\kappa_{i}\\
-\frac{1}{x_{t}-c_{i}}+\kappa_{i}&amp;\frac{1-\alpha_{i}}{(x_{t}-c_{i})^{2}}\\
\end{bmatrix}\end{array}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS7.p1.2" class="ltx_p">reveals that the objective is generally non-convex since the scaled determinant</p>
<table id="S3.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{t}-c_{i})^{2}\det(H)=\left(\frac{1}{\alpha_{i}}-\psi^{(1)}(\alpha_{i})\right)(1-\alpha_{i})-\left(\frac{1}{x_{t}-c_{i}}+\kappa_{i}\right)^{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{t}-c_{i})^{2}\det(H)=\left(\frac{1}{\alpha_{i}}-\psi^{(1)}(\alpha_{i})\right)(1-\alpha_{i})-\left(\frac{1}{x_{t}-c_{i}}+\kappa_{i}\right)^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS7.p1.3" class="ltx_p">is negative in the range of typical <math id="S3.SS7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> that we encounter. It is an open problem to see if nonlinear fitting <math id="S3.SS7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> in one step is faster than the ECM coordinate zig-zag<span id="footnote6" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">6</span>
              
              
              
            We currently only do one update of <math id="footnote6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="footnote6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> per EM-step, but updates could be done in any order or even repeatedly per step.</span></span></span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Hierarchical Modeling</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We motivate the use of gamma mixture models to characterize cosine similarity. One promising perspective arises from viewing the embedding space <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> through the lens of topic modeling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, where documents and queries are associated with a hierarchy of latent topics, and embeddings are organized around their respective topic centers. Under this view, cosine similarities are naturally right-skewed, as a given query tends to be close to a cluster center in the embedding space.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Simulation of Hierarchical Clustering Distribution (thanks to ChatGPT for explaining my code in <span id="alg1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_LaTeX_logo" style="letter-spacing:-0.2em; margin-right:0.1em;">L<span id="alg1.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:0.4ex;font-variant:small-caps;;">a</span>T<span id="alg1.5.2" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:-0.2ex;font-variant:small-caps;font-size:120%;">e</span>X</span>)</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.6" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
            
            
          Depth <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, Ratio <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math>, Degree <math id="alg1.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, Dimension <math id="alg1.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=384" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>384</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=384</annotation></semantics></math>, Seed <math id="alg1.l1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=1</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
            
            
          Set random seed to <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
            
            
          Initialize <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\leftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\leftarrow</annotation></semantics></math> one vector with entries from uniform<math id="alg1.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-1,1)</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="alg1.l3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
            
            
          <span id="alg1.l4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="alg1.l4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
            
            
            Initialize empty list <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
            
            
            <span id="alg1.l6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> each vector <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="alg1.l6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
            
            
             Sample <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> new vectors uniformly from <math id="alg1.l7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-1,1)^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-1,1)^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
            
            
             For each, compute <math id="alg1.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\leftarrow\eta\cdot x+\text{noise}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mtext>noise</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\leftarrow\eta\cdot x+\text{noise}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
            
            
             Append all <math id="alg1.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="alg1.l9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
            
            
            <span id="alg1.l10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l10.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">11:</span></span>
            
            
            Set <math id="alg1.l11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\leftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\leftarrow</annotation></semantics></math> concatenate all vectors in <math id="alg1.l11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l12.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">12:</span></span>
            
            
          <span id="alg1.l12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l12.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l13.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">13:</span></span>
            
            
          <span id="alg1.l13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> each vector <math id="alg1.l13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="alg1.l13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l13.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l14" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l14.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">14:</span></span>
            
            
            Normalize <math id="alg1.l14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> to unit length

</div>
<div id="alg1.l15" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l15.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">15:</span></span>
            
            
          <span id="alg1.l15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l15.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l16" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l16.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">16:</span></span>
            
            
          Let <math id="alg1.l16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\leftarrow X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\leftarrow X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> (the original vector)

</div>
<div id="alg1.l17" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l17.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">17:</span></span>
            
            
          <span id="alg1.l17.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> each <math id="alg1.l17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="alg1.l17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l17.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l18" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l18.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">18:</span></span>
            
            
            Compute <math id="alg1.l18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\leftarrow q\cdot x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\leftarrow q\cdot x</annotation></semantics></math> (cosine similarity)

</div>
<div id="alg1.l19" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l19.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">19:</span></span>
            
            
          <span id="alg1.l19.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l19.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l20" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l20.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">20:</span></span>
            
            
          <span id="alg1.l20.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="alg1.l20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To make this intuition precise, we turn to Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 4 Hierarchical Modeling ‣ Gamma Mixture Modeling for Cosine Similarity in Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, which constructs a hierarchical tree of cluster centers. At each iteration, a node in the binary tree splits into <span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">degree</span> child nodes, centered near their parent. The centers are perturbed according to a correlation strength parameter <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math>, creating a structured dependency across levels. This generative process induces a heavy right tail in the distribution of similarities: the cosine similarity between the query <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> and a sampled embedding <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> depends on their relative positions in the tree, i.e., how recently they share a common ancestor. When <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is drawn from a node closely related to <math id="S4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, the similarity is high; otherwise, it decays due to increasing separation in the latent space.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">With a binary tree, <span id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">degree</span> = 2, and sufficient <span id="S4.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">depth</span> (e.g., 20), we obtain enough samples to meaningfully study the distribution. For example, with <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=0.95" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.95</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=0.95</annotation></semantics></math>, the distribution is well-approximated by a single gamma (see Figure <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4 Hierarchical Modeling ‣ Gamma Mixture Modeling for Cosine Similarity in Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>). Increasing to <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=0.995" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.995</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=0.995</annotation></semantics></math>, a mixture of two gammas fits better, see Figure <a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 4 Hierarchical Modeling ‣ Gamma Mixture Modeling for Cosine Similarity in Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.05309v1/hierarchy_sim1.png" id="S4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:364/235;" width="364" height="235" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Histogram and fitted gamma for <math id="S4.F5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=0.95" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.95</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=0.95</annotation></semantics></math> in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 4 Hierarchical Modeling ‣ Gamma Mixture Modeling for Cosine Similarity in Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. It is fit well by a single gamma.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.05309v1/hierarchy_sim2.png" id="S4.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:363/237;" width="363" height="237" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>Histogram and fitted gamma mixture for <math id="S4.F6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=0.995" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.995</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=0.995</annotation></semantics></math> in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 4 Hierarchical Modeling ‣ Gamma Mixture Modeling for Cosine Similarity in Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. It is fit well by a mixture of two gammas.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Heuristic Argument – Mixture of Multiple Hierarchies</h3>

<figure id="S4.F7" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.05309v1/hierarchy_sim4.png" id="S4.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:313/269;" width="313" height="269" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span>Histograms for <math id="S4.F7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=0.95" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.95</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=0.95</annotation></semantics></math> in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 4 Hierarchical Modeling ‣ Gamma Mixture Modeling for Cosine Similarity in Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Colors indicate the hierarchy level whose center is closest to the query. The overlapping contributions from different levels blend to form a heavy right tail; Compare with Figure <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4 Hierarchical Modeling ‣ Gamma Mixture Modeling for Cosine Similarity in Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We base our heuristic argument on viewing the distribution in Figure <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4 Hierarchical Modeling ‣ Gamma Mixture Modeling for Cosine Similarity in Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> as a mixture of level-wise contributions from the tree generated by Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 4 Hierarchical Modeling ‣ Gamma Mixture Modeling for Cosine Similarity in Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Each successive level contains half as many nodes and thus contributes half as many samples, producing a geometric decay clearly visible in the color-coded breakdown of Figure <a href="#S4.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 4.1 Heuristic Argument – Mixture of Multiple Hierarchies ‣ 4 Hierarchical Modeling ‣ Gamma Mixture Modeling for Cosine Similarity in Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>. Empirically, each level’s component distribution is similarly shaped, shifts rightward in similarity space, and maintains comparable variance. This approximates, in the continuum, the convolution of an exponential decay with a more symmetric kernel<span id="footnote7" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">7</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">7</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">7</span>
              
              
              
            The kernel can be modeled as a gamma with large <math id="footnote7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, looking approximately Gaussian in this case.</span></span></span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Averaging over latent factors – such as depth, correlation strength, and topic context – across multiple instances of Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 4 Hierarchical Modeling ‣ Gamma Mixture Modeling for Cosine Similarity in Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, and their level-wise components produces a distribution shaped by overlapping exponential effects (with similar rates) and hidden variability. This blend naturally aligns with the behavior of a gamma mixture model and motivates its use as a flexible and interpretable fit.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Other Models and Data</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to the <span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_typewriter">all-MiniLM-L6-v2</span> sentence embedding based on MiniLM<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, we consider two other small language models based on MPNet<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> and RoBERTa<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt">model</th>
<th id="S5.p2.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">speed</th>
<th id="S5.p2.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">dimension</th>
<th id="S5.p2.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">layers</th>
<th id="S5.p2.1.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">context</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S5.p2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.p2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.p2.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_typewriter">all-MiniLM-L6-v2</span></th>
<td id="S5.p2.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t">1000</td>
<td id="S5.p2.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t">384</td>
<td id="S5.p2.1.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t">6</td>
<td id="S5.p2.1.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t">256</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.p2.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.p2.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_row"><span id="S5.p2.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_typewriter">all-mpnet-base-v2</span></th>
<td id="S5.p2.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">200</td>
<td id="S5.p2.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">768</td>
<td id="S5.p2.1.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">12</td>
<td id="S5.p2.1.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">384</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.p2.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.p2.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_row"><span id="S5.p2.1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_typewriter">all-roberta-large-v1</span></th>
<td id="S5.p2.1.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">80</td>
<td id="S5.p2.1.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">1024</td>
<td id="S5.p2.1.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">24</td>
<td id="S5.p2.1.4.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">128</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Speed is in sentences per second on a <span id="S5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_typewriter">Tesla V100-PCIE-16GB</span> GPU. The dimension is embedding dimension, and the context window is measured in tokens.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to the arXiv abstracts dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, we consider Wikipedia <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> and ag_news <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>. Consider the distributions in all 9 pairings of the 3 models with the 3 datasets in Figure <a href="#S5.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ 5 Other Models and Data ‣ Gamma Mixture Modeling for Cosine Similarity in Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>. These are formed by taking the first sentence in each dataset and computing the cosine similarity against the first 100K other sentences. They are all pretty well described by a single gamma distribution.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F8" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.05309v1/multiple_models_and_data.png" id="S5.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:509/515;" width="509" height="515" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8: </span>Distribution of cosine similarity per model and dataset, each fit with a single gamma.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Warm Starting Speed up</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We fit on a smaller set of data at first to warm start the convergence and to speed up the algorithm. We typically do this for 95% of the iterations on 1/20 of the data; only spending the last 5% of the iterations on the full data set. The difference in score is negligible, but the speed gain is an order of magnitude. Our C++ code is currently competitive with <span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">scipy.stats.gamma.fit</span>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S6.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt">algorithm</th>
<th id="S6.p2.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">states</th>
<th id="S6.p2.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">time(ms)</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S6.p2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S6.p2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">scipy.stats.gamma.fit</th>
<td id="S6.p2.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">1</td>
<td id="S6.p2.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">863</td></tr>
<tr id="S6.p2.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S6.p2.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">ours</th>
<td id="S6.p2.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">1</td>
<td id="S6.p2.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">116</td></tr>
<tr id="S6.p2.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S6.p2.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">ours</th>
<td id="S6.p2.1.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">2</td>
<td id="S6.p2.1.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">236</td></tr>
